WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.840
<v Maya>Hi everyone, and welcome to this study series on Cardio Regulations.

2
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.060
<v Maya>I'm Maya.

3
00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.410
<v Dev>And I'm Dev.

4
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:13.050
<v Dev>This is the series where we turn two lectures of cardio into something you can simply listen to.

5
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.340
<v Maya>That's the idea.

6
00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:21.220
<v Maya>You should be able to close the slides, put your headphones on, and by the end understand every concept, not just memorise it.

7
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.330
<v Maya>I explain, Dev asks the questions you're probably thinking, and every few minutes we stop and recap.

8
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.990
<v Dev>So what's the plan?

9
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.240
<v Maya>Six parts.

10
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.130
<v Maya>The first three cover the first cardio lecture.

11
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.260
<v Maya>Part one, today, is what cardio actually is, the two kinds of endurance, and aerobic cross training.

12
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:46.550
<v Maya>Part two is the classification of aerobic activities by joint stress, plus a quick revision of blood circulation.

13
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:58.010
<v Maya>Part three is the key terms and formulas, like heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output and VO₂ max, then workload, the long term benefits of cardio, and the class questions.

14
00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.710
<v Dev>And the second lecture?

15
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.190
<v Maya>Parts four to six.

16
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:09.900
<v Maya>Part four is intensity, the heart rate scale, the levels from beginner to super advanced, and the six factors a trainer uses to prescribe cardio.

17
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:18.200
<v Maya>Part five is calculating the target heart rate with two methods, why a lower resting heart rate is better, and the devices that monitor heart rate.

18
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:29.430
<v Maya>Part six is how to estimate intensity without devices, the rating of perceived exertion and the talk test, then the training methods, continuous, interval and Fartlek, and how to progress.

19
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.220
<v Maya>Part six ends with a twelve question quiz on everything.

20
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.090
<v Dev>That's a lot of numbers.

21
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.070
<v Maya>It's very logical, and we'll build it step by step.

22
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.450
<v Maya>Let's start at the beginning.

23
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.610
<v Maya>Dev, if I say cardio, what comes to your mind?

24
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.120
<v Dev>Jogging, treadmill, walking, cycling.

25
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.340
<v Dev>And honestly, losing weight.

26
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.200
<v Maya>Those activities are right.

27
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.540
<v Maya>And the weight loss answer is what most of the public thinks.

28
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:58.170
<v Maya>But from the course point of view, fat loss is not the result of any one type of training by itself.

29
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:03.750
<v Maya>Without a well balanced nutrition plan designed for fat loss, no training type produces it.

30
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:09.660
<v Maya>Cardio and weight training can facilitate fat loss, but they're not the tools that remove fat.

31
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.100
<v Dev>So what is cardio actually for?

32
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.100
<v Maya>Stamina.

33
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.780
<v Maya>And stamina is the same thing as endurance.

34
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.880
<v Maya>The word cardio comes from the Greek word cardium, which means heart.

35
00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.480
<v Maya>Think of cardiology and cardiologist.

36
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:28.250
<v Maya>So cardio training means training the heart for a purpose, and the purpose is building endurance.

37
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:33.850
<v Dev>So if someone asks for the main benefit of cardio, the answer is building endurance.

38
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.990
<v Maya>Exactly.

39
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.440
<v Maya>And two of the five components of fitness are about endurance.

40
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.110
<v Maya>Cardiorespiratory endurance, and muscular endurance.

41
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.790
<v Maya>Let's take them apart.

42
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.390
<v Maya>Forget the textbook definition for a moment.

43
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.200
<v Maya>Just take the word apart.

44
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:55.400
<v Maya>Endurance, also called stamina, is the ability of someone to do something for a long duration without getting fatigued.

45
00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.740
<v Maya>Someone, something.

46
00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.070
<v Maya>So for each type of endurance we ask three questions.

47
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.530
<v Maya>Who is the someone?

48
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.030
<v Maya>What is the something?

49
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.920
<v Maya>And how can it be improved?

50
00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.270
<v Dev>Who, what, how.

51
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.970
<v Dev>Okay, start with cardiorespiratory endurance.

52
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:10.870
<v Maya>Who?

53
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.890
<v Maya>The answer is hidden in the name.

54
00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.740
<v Maya>Cardio is heart, respiratory is lungs.

55
00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.360
<v Maya>So the heart and lungs.

56
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:18.250
<v Maya>What?

57
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.180
<v Maya>To deliver oxygenated blood to the working skeletal muscles.

58
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.650
<v Maya>Supply, deliver and transport all mean the same thing here.

59
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.670
<v Maya>And how?

60
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.270
<v Maya>By doing any type of aerobic activity.

61
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.090
<v Maya>Cycle, jog, walk, run, swim, anything aerobic.

62
00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.760
<v Dev>What does working skeletal muscles mean?

63
00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:38.880
<v Maya>Skeletal muscles are the ones attached to the bones.

64
00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.120
<v Maya>Working ones are the ones actually doing the activity.

65
00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.700
<v Maya>In jogging, that's mainly the leg muscles.

66
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:51.080
<v Maya>The heart and lungs deliver oxygenated blood to them, and the heart and lungs themselves should not get tired.

67
00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:57.410
<v Maya>If they start tiring during the task, that person needs to work on cardiorespiratory endurance.

68
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.620
<v Dev>And why is aerobic activity the right tool?

69
00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.250
<v Maya>Aerobic means in the presence of oxygen.

70
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:11.240
<v Maya>In the aerobic energy system, the mitochondria, the powerhouse of the cell, use oxygen to make ATP, which is usable energy.

71
00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.830
<v Maya>Aerobic activities are the ones you can do for more than three minutes non stop.

72
00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:22.990
<v Maya>Doing them creates a continuous demand for more and more oxygen, and the heart and lungs get better at supplying it.

73
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.940
<v Dev>Like supply and demand.

74
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.110
<v Dev>If there's no demand, the supply has no reason to improve.

75
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.130
<v Maya>Perfect.

76
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.980
<v Maya>Now muscular endurance.

77
00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:32.880
<v Maya>Who?

78
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.810
<v Maya>The working skeletal muscles.

79
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:35.690
<v Maya>What?

80
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.770
<v Maya>To contract sub maximally.

81
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.510
<v Maya>Maximally means one hundred percent effort.

82
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.860
<v Maya>The prefix sub means under, so sub maximally means contracting with less than one hundred percent effort.

83
00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:47.870
<v Maya>And how?

84
00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.880
<v Maya>By doing task specific aerobic activity.

85
00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:52.610
<v Dev>Why task specific?

86
00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.140
<v Dev>It's the same leg muscles in jogging and cycling.

87
00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.460
<v Maya>Same muscles, different movement pattern.

88
00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:08.450
<v Maya>If your muscles are trained all year to contract sub maximally in the jogging pattern, you can't expect them to perform equally well in a cycling tournament at the end of the year.

89
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.620
<v Maya>To improve muscular endurance for jogging, jog.

90
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.220
<v Maya>For cycling, cycle.

91
00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.800
<v Maya>For swimming, swim.

92
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.090
<v Dev>Example?

93
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.380
<v Maya>Usain Bolt and Michael Phelps.

94
00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.940
<v Maya>Bolt can't swim like Phelps, and Phelps can't run like Bolt.

95
00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:28.220
<v Maya>Both have very well trained hearts and lungs, so both have top notch cardiorespiratory endurance.

96
00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:32.130
<v Maya>But their muscular endurance is excellent only in their own field.

97
00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:34.790
<v Maya>Here's the analogy that makes the difference stick.

98
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.070
<v Maya>Think of a car.

99
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.820
<v Maya>The engine has one job, give the power to move the car from point A to point B, whatever the terrain.

100
00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.430
<v Maya>Desert, snow, hills, a normal road.

101
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.610
<v Maya>The heart and lungs are the engine.

102
00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.260
<v Maya>They do the same job for any activity.

103
00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.770
<v Dev>And the tyres?

104
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.460
<v Maya>The tyres can't be the same for all terrain.

105
00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.510
<v Maya>For snow you wrap chains around them, or you use an all terrain vehicle.

106
00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.270
<v Maya>The tyres are the muscles.

107
00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:05.690
<v Maya>The heart and lungs supply blood for anything, but the muscles must be trained differently for each activity.

108
00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:11.250
<v Dev>So engine is cardiorespiratory endurance, tyres are muscular endurance.

109
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.810
<v Dev>And cardio training builds both.

110
00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:14.700
<v Maya>Yes.

111
00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:15.870
<v Maya>Quick recap.

112
00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.730
<v Maya>Cardio comes from the word for heart, and its purpose is endurance.

113
00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:27.750
<v Maya>Cardiorespiratory endurance, heart and lungs, deliver oxygenated blood, any aerobic activity, not task specific.

114
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:34.050
<v Maya>Muscular endurance, working muscles, contract sub maximally, task specific aerobic activity.

115
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.540
<v Maya>Let's pause and think.

116
00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:36.740
<v Maya>Question one.

117
00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:41.140
<v Maya>A client trains only on a treadmill all year and then joins a swimming race.

118
00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:44.910
<v Maya>Which endurance is likely to transfer well, and which is not?

119
00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:52.320
<v Dev>Cardiorespiratory endurance should transfer, because the heart and lungs do the same job for any activity.

120
00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.740
<v Dev>The muscular endurance won't transfer fully, because swimming uses a different movement pattern.

121
00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.980
<v Maya>Now, who needs task specific muscular endurance?

122
00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:06.160
<v Maya>People whose daily life uses the muscles in the same movement pattern again and again.

123
00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:11.270
<v Maya>A cricket batsman perfecting one shot, who practises that shot for hours in the nets.

124
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.610
<v Maya>A tennis player practising a forehand.

125
00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.320
<v Maya>A carpenter cutting wood.

126
00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.860
<v Maya>A junior chef rolling out chapatis day after day.

127
00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.370
<v Dev>But most of our clients aren't like that.

128
00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:22.260
<v Maya>Right.

129
00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.390
<v Maya>Most future clients are general lifestyle people.

130
00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.330
<v Maya>A homemaker, a business owner, a student, a corporate employee.

131
00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:35.420
<v Maya>Their day is wake up, work or college, meetings, lunch, maybe a workout two or three times a week, then home.

132
00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.170
<v Maya>Their muscles don't move in one pattern for long durations.

133
00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:40.980
<v Dev>So what do they need?

134
00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:45.790
<v Maya>Muscular endurance that is complete and overall, not specific to one task.

135
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.950
<v Maya>It's called comprehensive muscular endurance, a three hundred and sixty degree endurance.

136
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.240
<v Maya>And the technique to build it is aerobic cross training.

137
00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.310
<v Dev>What does cross training mean?

138
00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.290
<v Maya>Doing multiple activities and not sticking to one.

139
00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.510
<v Maya>Athletes do it a lot to get better in their main sport.

140
00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.800
<v Maya>Add the word aerobic, and it means doing multiple aerobic activities, not just one.

141
00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:12.100
<v Maya>Cricketers who post videos of sprinting and weightlifting are cross training.

142
00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:19.560
<v Maya>Sprinting helps a veteran batsman run fast between the wickets, and lifting builds the power to hit the ball over the boundary.

143
00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:24.480
<v Dev>A student might ask whether cross training is the same as functional training.

144
00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:26.960
<v Maya>The lecture says no, not for this topic.

145
00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:36.830
<v Maya>Functional training and things like HYROX mix anaerobic and aerobic work, for example a sled push, and many parts can't be done for more than three minutes non stop.

146
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.430
<v Maya>Here we stay in the purely aerobic zone, with no weights.

147
00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.360
<v Maya>There are two ways to apply aerobic cross training.

148
00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:48.430
<v Maya>Way one, do multiple aerobic activities, either within a week or within a session.

149
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.490
<v Maya>Within a week, for example, cycling on Monday, a run on Wednesday, a swim on Friday.

150
00:08:53.540 --> 00:09:01.830
<v Maya>Within a session, split the time, for example a thirty minute session of ten minutes jogging, ten minutes on the cross trainer and ten minutes rowing.

151
00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.270
<v Dev>And way two?

152
00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:07.080
<v Maya>Do one activity that uses the upper and lower body together.

153
00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.450
<v Maya>That's why the elliptical machine is called the cross trainer.

154
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:14.600
<v Maya>Two pedals connected to two handles, so hands and legs move together.

155
00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:20.010
<v Maya>And these numbers are only hypothetical examples to explain the idea, not a prescription.

156
00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:27.420
<v Maya>The slides use sixty minute sessions and twenty minute blocks, the E-book uses twenty minute blocks, so check your course material.

157
00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.950
<v Dev>Why does cross training actually help?

158
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.670
<v Dev>What are the benefits?

159
00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.270
<v Maya>The lecture gives three.

160
00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.240
<v Maya>Benefit one, capillarization over a larger surface area.

161
00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:43.940
<v Maya>Capillaries are the tiniest blood vessels, where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged with the cells.

162
00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:48.190
<v Maya>Cardio builds new capillaries, which is called neocapillarization.

163
00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.310
<v Maya>If you only do a lower body activity, capillaries improve only there.

164
00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.690
<v Maya>With upper, lower and combined activities, they spread across the body.

165
00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:58.550
<v Dev>Is there an analogy?

166
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.360
<v Maya>Mobile network towers.

167
00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.930
<v Maya>A company doesn't get good coverage by putting more and more towers in one place.

168
00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.370
<v Maya>Coverage is best when towers are evenly distributed.

169
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.100
<v Maya>Same with capillaries.

170
00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:11.630
<v Dev>Benefit two?

171
00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.590
<v Maya>Avoiding overuse injuries.

172
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:20.640
<v Maya>Someone who only cycles puts all the work on the same joints, knee, ankle and hip, the same bones and the same tendons.

173
00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.080
<v Maya>Like a machine, it gets wear and tear.

174
00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.770
<v Maya>When the whole body is recruited, the responsibility is shared.

175
00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.040
<v Dev>And three?

176
00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.360
<v Maya>Boosting interest, which is about the client, not the trainer.

177
00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:35.950
<v Maya>Gym memberships peak on the first of January, and new members trust every word.

178
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:42.100
<v Maya>But after about a month, if cardio means only the treadmill for thirty or forty minutes, monotony arrives.

179
00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:45.250
<v Maya>Excuses start showing up only on cardio days.

180
00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:54.700
<v Maya>With variety, there is a surprise element in the week, and within a session the client keeps looking forward to the next activity, so thirty minutes pass without noticing.

181
00:10:54.750 --> 00:11:01.030
<v Dev>The E-book gives four benefits, including recruiting more muscles, and no capillarization.

182
00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.130
<v Maya>Good catch, so check your course material.

183
00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.990
<v Maya>Time for the closing recap.

184
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.510
<v Maya>Cardio means heart training, and its purpose is endurance.

185
00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.630
<v Maya>Cardiorespiratory endurance is heart and lungs, any aerobic activity.

186
00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.310
<v Maya>Muscular endurance is task specific.

187
00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:26.950
<v Maya>Most clients need comprehensive muscular endurance, built with aerobic cross training, in two ways, multiple activities, or an activity using upper and lower body together.

188
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:33.450
<v Dev>And the three benefits are larger capillary coverage, fewer overuse injuries, and better interest.

189
00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.510
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

190
00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:39.470
<v Maya>One, cardio builds endurance, and fat loss needs nutrition.

191
00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:44.650
<v Maya>Two, cardiorespiratory endurance is the engine, any aerobic activity.

192
00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.130
<v Maya>Three, muscular endurance is the tyres, task specific.

193
00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.050
<v Maya>Four, comprehensive endurance comes from aerobic cross training.

194
00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:56.030
<v Maya>Five, the three benefits, towers, wear and tear, and interest.

195
00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.300
<v Dev>Next time, we classify aerobic activities by how they stress your joints.

196
00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:02.580
<v Maya>See you in part two.

